if (cmd->fail_io) {
    WRITE_UNLOCK(cmd);
    goto out;
}
```
Reason: The `WRITE_LOCK` macro unconditionally returns from the function when the root lock cannot be acquired (e.g., because the block manager is read‑only or `fail_io` is set). This early return skips the cleanup code at the `out` label that frees the temporary block manager `new_bm`, causing a memory leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
#define WRITE_LOCK_OR_GOTO(cmd, label) do { if (!cmd_write_lock((cmd))) goto label; } while (0)
...
WRITE_LOCK_OR_GOTO(cmd, out);
```
Reason: The new macro `WRITE_LOCK_OR_GOTO` jumps to the specified label (here `out`) when the lock cannot be acquired, instead of returning immediately. This ensures that the clean‑up code at `out` (which frees `new_bm`) is executed, thereby preventing the memory leak.

